  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nFor the rest of this afternoon, lingering stratus clouds will \nslowly scatter across the Northland, leaving behind calm and \nquiet conditions. Cloud cover will increase again tonight across\nthe area. Opted to increase low temperatures for tonight\ncompared to guidance due to higher cloud cover limiting heat \nrelease overnight, with readings staying in the lower to mid 30s\nrather than dipping into the 20s.\n\nAn upper-level cutoff low over the Four Corners will phase into an\nincoming trough from the Canadian Prairies on Thursday, leading\nto a surface low moving across Wisconsin into the Great Lakes. \nRainfall will develop along a frontogenesis band Thursday \nmorning and spread across the area through the afternoon. Rain \nchances will be highest across northwest Wisconsin, where a \ntenth to a quarter inch of rain is expected. Low temperatures in\nthe mid 30s are also anticipated for tomorrow night due to \nhigher cloud cover.\n\nA large trough will bring rain chances and cool air close behind for\nthe late week. Precipitation along and wrap around precipitation\nbehind the front will keep rain chances going for Thursday night\ninto Friday morning. There is no snow currently in the latest \nforecast for early Friday morning, but a few flakes falling with\nlight rain is still possible across the Arrowhead and south \nshore. Strong northwest winds will surge behind the boundary \nlate Thursday night into Friday, gusting 25 to 35 mph across the\nNorthland, with gusts up to 45 mph near Lake Superior before \ndiminishing Friday afternoon.\n\nHigh pressure builds across the Upper Midwest Friday night into\nSaturday, setting the stage for very dry air to overspread the\nregion. Forecasters are still looking at relative humidity as low as\n25 percent for Saturday. Winds are expected to increase in the\nafternoon, but timing will depend on the speed of a warm front moving\nthrough. The combination of gusty southwest winds reaching 20 to 30\nmph and the dry air mass could lead to near-critical to critical fire\nweather conditions on Saturday.\n\nAn upper-level shortwave will pass north of the international border\non Sunday, dragging a mostly dry cold front through our region with\npassing clouds. Heading into early next week, upper-level ridging\nwill become the predominant pattern driver across the Great Lakes.\nThis will maintain dry, mostly clear, and mild conditions through\nMonday and Tuesday. While a couple of weak shortwaves may attempt to\nbring a stray shower next week, dry weather is favored to\ndominate.",
